-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTES THAT MATTER, AND ARE NOT PART OF THE LICENCE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. VERSIONS PUBLISHED BEFORE THIS CHANGE REMAIN UNDER GPL-3.0-or-later.

   VeilVoice up to and including v0.1.9 was released under the GNU General
   Public License, version 3 or later. That grant was made and cannot be
   withdrawn: anyone who received one of those releases keeps every right the
   GPL gave them, including commercial use, for those versions. This licence
   governs releases made after the change and nothing before it.

2. THIS IS NOT AN OPEN SOURCE LICENCE, AND SAYING SO WOULD BE FALSE.

   The NonCommercial term fails both the Open Source Definition and the Free
   Software Foundation's definition of free software, because both require the
   freedom to use the work for any purpose, commercial purposes included.

   VeilVoice is therefore SOURCE-AVAILABLE: every line can be read, built,
   audited, modified and redistributed non-commercially, and every claim it
   makes can still be checked by anybody. What it is not is "libre", "free
   software" or "open source", and this project does not use those words about
   itself any more. Describing it with them would be the same kind of overclaim
   the rest of this repository exists to avoid.

   A practical consequence, stated rather than discovered: most Linux
   distributions will not package NonCommercial software in their main
   repositories.

3. THE DEPENDENCIES KEEP THEIR OWN LICENCES.

   Every third-party crate in this project is under a permissive licence
   (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, BSL, CC0, Zlib or Unicode-3.0). Those licences
   are unaffected by this one and continue to govern that code, including their
   own attribution requirements.

   Note also that this licence cannot be combined with GPL code in either
   direction. Audacity (GPL-2.0-or-later) is recommended and integrated with;
   its source is never copied in. That rule has not changed, only its reason.

4. CREATIVE COMMONS DOES NOT RECOMMEND ITS LICENCES FOR SOFTWARE.

   Creative Commons says so itself, on the ground that CC licences do not
   contain the source-code and distribution terms software licences usually
   carry. This licence was chosen with that known and is recorded here rather
   than left for a reader to discover.
